# SELinux types for Corium's own state.
#
# This module declares types and nothing else. It confines no process: there is
# no domain here, no transition, and no allow rule. corium-apid and
# corium-agent run as unconfined_service_t like every other service on this
# image, which is the same position corium-agent has always been in.
#
# What it buys is a name. The operator CA a node obeys and the private key it
# serves with are not ordinary machine state, and giving them a type of their
# own means a confined domain -- written later, on a machine, from real AVC
# denials rather than from guesses -- has something to hang rules on. Without
# it, that work would start by relabelling files on nodes already in service.
#
# Note what is deliberately *not* labelled: /var/lib/corium itself. Five things
# read that directory, and two of them are outside this project's control --
# systemd evaluating ConditionPathExists= for corium-bootstrap.service and
# corium-uncordon.service, and the greenboot health check reading
# `bootstrapped`. Getting the greenboot one wrong does not fail visibly: it
# fails the check, and three failed checks roll the node back to its previous
# OS image. A relabelling that can cause fleet-wide rollbacks is a poor trade
# for a label, so the parent keeps var_lib_t and only the subtree that nothing
# else reads is named here.

policy_module(corium, 1.0.0)

########################################
#
# Declarations
#

# The node's management state: /var/lib/corium/api.
#
# files_type() is what makes this usable as a file type at all -- it adds the
# file_type and non_security_file_type attributes that the rest of the policy
# reasons about. Unconfined domains reach every file_type, which is why
# labelling this changes nothing today and is safe to ship ahead of the policy
# that will use it.
type corium_api_var_lib_t;
files_type(corium_api_var_lib_t)
